WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.250
Welcome to Building Azure Security,
Chapter 7: Private Link, DNS and Key Vault.

2
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.462
A private endpoint is an address.

3
00:00:08.462 --> 00:00:11.537
Whether anything uses
it is a DNS question,

4
00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:15.226
and whether the public path
is closed is a third one.

5
00:00:15.226 --> 00:00:16.975
The code asks all three.

6
00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:21.135
We'll start from Microsoft Learn's
Private Link commands for Key Vault,

7
00:00:21.135 --> 00:00:26.012
the zone group, the Cloud Adoption Framework's
central-zone pattern and Learn's Key

8
00:00:26.012 --> 00:00:28.952
Vault recommendations, see
what they leave to you,

9
00:00:28.952 --> 00:00:32.825
read the kit's check, and walk two
labs, the evidence and the dangers.

10
00:00:32.825 --> 00:00:35.510
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved,

11
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:39.830
and the code is illustrative,
tested against a fictional estate.

12
00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.955
Why three questions?

13
00:00:44.955 --> 00:00:49.033
Because a private endpoint that exists
can still go unused if the name

14
00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:53.393
doesn't resolve to it, and can still be
bypassed if the public path stays open.

15
00:00:53.393 --> 00:00:56.349
By the end of this chapter
you can answer four things:

16
00:00:56.349 --> 00:00:59.993
whether every privatelink zone is
central and linked to the hub,

17
00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:04.049
whether every private endpoint
registers its record in a central zone,

18
00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:07.968
which resources still accept public
traffic beside their endpoint,

19
00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:12.368
and which key vaults miss any part of
the posture Microsoft Learn recommends.

20
00:01:12.368 --> 00:01:15.055
And you can compute the
guide's vault-posture metric.

21
00:01:15.055 --> 00:01:18.833
It's for platform owners, who
own the zones; workload owners,

22
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:23.360
who own endpoints and vaults; and
the developers who review both.

23
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:28.985
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

24
00:01:28.985 --> 00:01:34.001
Learn's Key Vault Private Link page creates
a vault on role-based access with purge

25
00:01:34.001 --> 00:01:39.017
protection, closes its firewall, creates
the vault's privatelink zone and its link,

26
00:01:39.017 --> 00:01:42.410
and the private endpoint,
then validates with nslookup.

27
00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:46.122
It also says name resolution is
where private endpoints fail.

28
00:01:46.122 --> 00:01:49.774
A private DNS zone group
registers the endpoint's record,

29
00:01:49.774 --> 00:01:51.872
and removes it with the endpoint.

30
00:01:51.872 --> 00:01:55.481
The Cloud Adoption Framework
keeps privatelink zones central

31
00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:59.159
in the connectivity subscription
and adds zone groups by policy.

32
00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:02.510
The zone group API
returns each group's zone.

33
00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:07.537
And Learn's Key Vault recommendations:
role-based access, not access policies;

34
00:02:07.537 --> 00:02:13.390
purge protection; private endpoints with
public access disabled; and audit logging.

35
00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.503
What does Learn leave to you?

36
00:02:18.503 --> 00:02:22.803
The commands make one zone, one
link, one endpoint; in practice,

37
00:02:22.803 --> 00:02:27.790
zones still get created in workload
subscriptions, and records split.

38
00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:30.522
A zone group per endpoint
means an endpoint

39
00:02:30.522 --> 00:02:33.178
without one has no record anybody manages.

40
00:02:33.178 --> 00:02:37.225
"Turn public access off once the
private path works" leaves room

41
00:02:37.225 --> 00:02:41.039
for the private path to work
while the public one stays open.

42
00:02:41.039 --> 00:02:43.758
Key Vault's recommendations are read once,

43
00:02:43.758 --> 00:02:46.553
and a vault created next
quarter misses one.

44
00:02:46.553 --> 00:02:50.727
And nslookup has to run from inside
a network, which the kit can't do.

45
00:02:50.727 --> 00:02:56.530
So the kit checks the records' configuration
every run, and Lab 2 runs nslookup.

46
00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.242
Six rules cover the network side.

47
00:03:02.242 --> 00:03:07.225
A privatelink zone outside the connectivity
subscription, or not linked to the hub,

48
00:03:07.225 --> 00:03:11.268
is a finding, as the Cloud Adoption
Framework's central pattern asks.

49
00:03:11.268 --> 00:03:14.948
A private endpoint with no zone
group, or one that registers

50
00:03:14.948 --> 00:03:18.780
in a zone outside the connectivity
subscription, is a finding.

51
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.842
A resource with a private endpoint and
public network access still on is a finding.

52
00:03:23.842 --> 00:03:28.985
And a platform service that's public with
no private endpoint at all is a warning,

53
00:03:28.985 --> 00:03:33.503
not a finding: the guide's private PaaS
target is the agency's own, rising,

54
00:03:33.503 --> 00:03:38.255
and a Front Door origin locked by Chapter
5's checks may be public by design.

55
00:03:38.255 --> 00:03:40.540
Vaults go to the Key Vault rules.

56
00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.996
Then every vault gets six checks from
Learn's Key Vault recommendations:

57
00:03:48.996 --> 00:03:52.682
role-based access, not access
policies; purge protection;

58
00:03:52.682 --> 00:03:57.467
a firewall whose default is deny; public
network access, which is a warning;

59
00:03:57.467 --> 00:04:02.565
an approved private endpoint; and audit
events sent to the platform workspace.

60
00:04:02.565 --> 00:04:07.176
A vault has the full posture when it
has role-based access, purge protection,

61
00:04:07.176 --> 00:04:09.928
an approved private
endpoint and audit logs:

62
00:04:09.928 --> 00:04:12.977
the four parts of the
guide's vault-posture metric.

63
00:04:12.977 --> 00:04:16.875
Key rotation policies are read
through the vault's own data plane,

64
00:04:16.875 --> 00:04:18.390
which the kit doesn't call.

65
00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:21.400
Lab 2 reads one with the guide's command.

66
00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.200
Who's involved?

67
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.878
The platform owner owns the central zones,

68
00:04:28.878 --> 00:04:32.550
their links to the hub, and the
policy that adds zone groups.

69
00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.622
Workload owners own their
endpoints and vaults,

70
00:04:35.622 --> 00:04:38.925
and close the public path
once the private one works.

71
00:04:38.925 --> 00:04:43.350
And the application owner keeps each
vault's posture, with the platform owner.

72
00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.965
Every read the check makes is Reader,

73
00:04:45.965 --> 00:04:50.500
and it never touches a vault's
data plane: no secrets, no keys.

74
00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:59.550
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with Pester, in
the kit's folder, and no subscription.

75
00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.817
Contoso's vault zone is
central and linked to the hub;

76
00:05:02.817 --> 00:05:07.375
the payroll team created its own blob
zone in its workload subscription.

77
00:05:07.375 --> 00:05:11.650
Step 1 runs the check: one of
three vaults has the full posture.

78
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.825
Step 2 reads each vault.

79
00:05:13.825 --> 00:05:15.575
The payroll vault has everything.

80
00:05:15.575 --> 00:05:20.575
The hub certificates vault has no private
endpoint, and its public access is on.

81
00:05:20.575 --> 00:05:26.250
And the portal vault misses every part:
access policies, no purge protection,

82
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:31.030
an open firewall, public access,
no endpoint and no audit log.

83
00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:39.255
Step 3 reads each private endpoint
and the zone it registers in.

84
00:05:39.255 --> 00:05:42.555
The vault's endpoint registers
in the central vault zone.

85
00:05:42.555 --> 00:05:46.180
The blob endpoint registers in
the payroll team's own zone.

86
00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:50.755
And the queue endpoint has no zone group
at all: nothing registers its record.

87
00:05:50.755 --> 00:05:52.880
Step 4 reads the findings.

88
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.971
The blob zone is in the payroll
subscription and not linked to the hub;

89
00:05:56.971 --> 00:06:01.205
the blob endpoint registers there;
the queue endpoint has no zone group;

90
00:06:01.205 --> 00:06:05.080
the storage account has two
endpoints and public access still on.

91
00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:09.567
Then the vault findings, and warnings
for public services with no endpoint.

92
00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:12.640
Step 5 runs this chapter's two tests.

93
00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:21.014
Lab 2 moves to a test subscription with
a test vault behind a private endpoint

94
00:06:21.014 --> 00:06:25.565
and the central zone, and a test machine
in a linked network, never production.

95
00:06:25.565 --> 00:06:31.078
Sign in, write the settings, and run the
check live, allowing the unverified calls.

96
00:06:31.078 --> 00:06:36.287
Compare with the guide's Validate query,
which reads the vault's role-based access,

97
00:06:36.287 --> 00:06:39.241
purge protection, firewall
and public access,

98
00:06:39.241 --> 00:06:42.740
and read one key's rotation
policy, which the kit doesn't.

99
00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.568
Then, from the test machine,
look up the vault's name:

100
00:06:45.568 --> 00:06:50.328
the answer should come through the vault's
privatelink zone with a private address.

101
00:06:50.328 --> 00:06:54.209
From outside, the same name
resolves to the public frontend,

102
00:06:54.209 --> 00:06:55.952
which refuses the request.

103
00:06:55.952 --> 00:06:59.140
Back out by deleting the
output and signing out.

104
00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.555
Each output is evidence
for something specific,

105
00:07:05.555 --> 00:07:10.952
from the guide's requirements
table against NIST SP 800-53.

106
00:07:10.952 --> 00:07:15.722
Central zones linked to the hub, every
endpoint registered there, and nothing public

107
00:07:15.722 --> 00:07:20.490
behind an endpoint support boundary
protection and information flow enforcement.

108
00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:25.440
Public services without an endpoint,
named, support boundary protection.

109
00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.901
And every vault on role-based
access with purge protection,

110
00:07:28.901 --> 00:07:33.280
a private endpoint and audit logs
supports cryptographic key management,

111
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:37.165
protection of information at rest,
and authenticator management.

112
00:07:37.165 --> 00:07:41.215
The kit's output supports these
controls; the assessor decides.

113
00:07:41.215 --> 00:07:45.796
The key security indicators are
FedRAMP's indicators for cloud providers,

114
00:07:45.796 --> 00:07:49.030
which the agency lines
its own evidence up with.

115
00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:54.180
What breaks in production?

116
00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.708
Zones in workload subscriptions
split the records,

117
00:07:57.708 --> 00:08:01.318
so resolution fails or goes
public; the kit flags them.

118
00:08:01.318 --> 00:08:06.180
An endpoint with no zone group has a record
somebody made by hand, or none at all.

119
00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:10.040
The public path left open means
the private endpoint works,

120
00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.955
and so does the internet;
three rules catch it.

121
00:08:12.955 --> 00:08:16.114
Purge protection can't be
turned off once it's on,

122
00:08:16.114 --> 00:08:19.517
which is the point, so the
kit only ever asks for it on.

123
00:08:19.517 --> 00:08:24.406
And configuration isn't resolution: the
records can exist while clients still

124
00:08:24.406 --> 00:08:29.580
resolve the public name, which is why Lab
2 runs nslookup from a linked machine.

125
00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:34.680
Here's what done looks like.

126
00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:39.217
Every privatelink zone is in the connectivity
subscription and linked to the hub.

127
00:08:39.217 --> 00:08:42.693
Every private endpoint has a
zone group in a central zone.

128
00:08:42.693 --> 00:08:46.741
No resource has an endpoint with
public network access still on,

129
00:08:46.741 --> 00:08:48.880
unless a decision record says why.

130
00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.155
Every vault has the full
posture, or a dated plan.

131
00:08:52.155 --> 00:08:55.905
And nslookup from a linked machine
returned a private address.

132
00:08:55.905 --> 00:08:59.955
Next, Chapter 8: the policy that
holds all of this in place,

133
00:08:59.955 --> 00:09:02.030
and the evidence that it did.
